  "total": 50,
  "toyota_summary": "The European Union is tightening its grip on where carmakers can source components and materials. The Industrial Policy Act pushes manufacturers to source green-transition products from within the EU rather than China, while Germany is now pushing for explicit \"made in Europe\" requirements in the framework. Starting January 2026, the carbon border adjustment mechanism will impose costs on imported parts based on their production emissions, effectively penalizing non-EU suppliers. These measures are fragmenting the global supply chain at a moment when the industry needs scale to compete.\n\nThe risk is stark: automakers face a choice between accepting higher costs to localize production in Europe, or accepting tariffs and market access complications. The EU is betting it can protect its manufacturers while accelerating the green transition, but the policy is already straining trade relations with the UK and raising costs for Japanese and other foreign suppliers. Germany's push for explicit regional content requirements suggests the protectionism will deepen, not ease.\n\nWatch whether carmakers begin announcing new European battery or semiconductor plants in response, or whether they start shifting investment to lower-cost regions entirely.",
